            <ArticleTitle>Correlation between lung perfusion blood volume and SPECT images in patients with chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension by balloon pulmonary angioplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>80-86</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-7071(17)30219-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinimag.2017.11.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We evaluated the reperfusion by balloon pulmonary angioplasty (BPA) in lung PBV and SPECT images.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In total, 17 patients and 57 sessions were evaluated. Pre-BPA and post-BPA lung PBV and SPECT/CT images, based on both anatomical segments and physiologic regions (upper/middle/lower) were compared.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">BPA had a positive effect on most hypoperfused/unperfused segments/regions. There was generally a high rate of agreement between PBV measurements and SPECT/CT.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">BPA shows promise as a treatment modality for CTEPH patients. SPECT/CT, and, to a lesser extent, PBV, were useful in indicating areas in need of balloon angioplasty.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
